[Music]  horn in Cherry Hill New Jersey outside  of Philadelphia a chef Greg Burnett grew  up in a true food family his grandfather  owned a butcher shop in a market and his  mother ran a restaurant of her own  after earning culinary and hospitality  degrees he worked for renowned chef Jean  George first at his New York restaurants  then as a chef trainer around the globe  before becoming executive chef at New  York's Tocqueville in 2012 he came back  to Philadelphia opening Vernick Food &amp;  Drink celebrated for no-fuss new  American cooking this year the  Philadelphia Inquirer called it the best  restaurant in the city and Vernick just  took home the 2017 James Beard Award for  Best Chef in the mid-atlantic region  chef Greg verdict welcome to the dish  congratulations thank you very much no  come on with it if you like to get that  it felt a little crazy you know I didn't  exactly know what to do when you're up  there I kind of wish I could hit rewind  and do it again cuz I'm getting made fun  of a lot for my little little speech I  made but it was great it was an honor  that's genuine emotion when you sit  there what is this wonderful meal that  we have here so we did sort of like a  little summer picnic situation I think  we need to start with a cocktail first  if we do right we did like our summer  watermelon cocktail with ginger and gin  and some cucumber and fresh lime juice  and there's remoteness as well and over  here because I'm taking it all yeah just  like very really nice fresh cocktail  yeah and then we have a couple sort of  summery salad some grilled romaine with  stone fruits quinoa salad with grilled  corn and blue cheese a beautiful tomato  tart and then whole roasted leg of lamb  with mustard and anchovy and then last  we did a cannoli stuffed with strawberry  mousse last but certainly not least chef  Alina I mean we could start with that if  you want we oftentimes we do you started  as a kid that really just grew up in a  kitchen in restaurants I mentioned the  background how did that shape you being  like that I mean it was it was my  childhood it's a lot of my memory  revolves around being in my mom's  restaurant she had like a little  luncheonette and my grand  father and I would be playing in the  back making little sandwiches and salads  and they all were disgusting but he  would eat them and I would like sample  him on them and then before that my  grandfather had a butcher shop in  Philadelphia so it's it's a little bit  of third-generation food business so you  know it's been really nice and when you  were a teenager your parents said you're  not going to camp you're gonna get a job  yeah yeah I wasn't happy about that I  just remember like I was 13 and they  were they were like you can't go to camp  anymore you need to get a job and so we  we made up like a fake resume with my  grades and like my activities and I  liked basketball and stuff and we drove  up and down the Jersey Shore just  resumes and finally somebody like  accepted it and interviewed me on the  spot and you know I don't know if I  should say this but I lied to them I  said I know how to cook and I had no  idea I got back in the car and I was  like mom I got a job and she's like I  told her the story and she's like what  you lied so yeah so I guess it worked  out great but I also a little life  lesson at the same time you ended up  working with John George training chefs  all around the world yeah what was that  experience like it was amazing I mean I  felt like for a moment I the greatest  chef job in the world in a lot of ways I  was a fly on the wall because you get to  see you know a hotel come to life not  just a restaurant but you get to sort of  be a fly on the wall and see everything  happen it's a great business education  if you yeah you know it was it was  amazing and at the same time I was sort  of building a curriculum as to what it  would take to open my own restaurant  so you know it was a lot of hotel out of  time on the road in hotels but um you  know every opening we got a little bit  better and I got a little more  experienced and to the point where I was  like you know I think I could do this  myself and you did and you and your wife  Julie back in Philadelphia why was it so  important for you to go back home and do  this Philadelphia's home it's always  been home  it was always sort of a conversation we  this is it's the third city we lived in  I love living in cities I love New York  I  Boston but there is no like sense of  home like Philadelphia was and now we  have a family there and it's just it  just is a special place to us it really  does feel like home well it sounds like  you have a special place there now given  what's happening with your restaurants  we're having a lot of fun that's great  it is the dish so as I have you sign it  let me ask you if you could have a meal  with anyone pastor present who would it  be I really think it'd be cool to have  all of like my past mentors sit down  with me and sort of break bread people  like John George and Greg brain in and  mark la Pico and Ken Oringer sort of a  small group of people who like taught me  the ways I'd be amazing maybe have  everybody cook a little something too  and what I became like you know thanks  to you guys that's right yeah chef Greg  verdict thank you thank you guys very  much and for more on Greg Vernick and  the dish you can head to our website at  CBS this morning dot-com

